<repaired_code>
def data(self, *keys):
    """
    Return the keys and values of this record as a dictionary,
    optionally including only certain values by index or key. Keys
    provided in the items that are not in the record will be
    inserted with a value of :const:`None`; indexes provided
    that are out of bounds will trigger an :exc:`IndexError`.

    :param keys: indexes or keys of the items to include; if none
                  are provided, all values will be included
    :return: dictionary of values, keyed by field name
    :raises: :exc:`IndexError` if an out-of-bounds index is specified
    """
    result = {}
    
    # If no keys provided, return all items
    if not keys:
        return dict(self.items())
    
    # Process provided keys
    for key in keys:
        if isinstance(key, int):
            # Handle index-based access
            try:
                items = list(self.items())
                field_name, value = items[key]
                result[field_name] = value
            except IndexError:
                raise IndexError(f"Index {key} is out of bounds")
        else:
            # Handle key-based access
            result[key] = dict(self.items()).get(key, None)
    
    return result
</repaired_code>

This solution:
1. Creates a dictionary to store the results
2. Handles the case when no keys are provided by returning all items
3. Processes both integer indexes and string keys
4. Raises IndexError for out-of-bounds indexes
5. Sets None for non-existent keys
6. Uses the items() method from the context to access record data
7. Returns a dictionary as specified in the docstring

The code now properly implements the functionality described in the docstring and handles all the required cases while fixing the original NoneType error.

